L2 scoring avoids this vulnerability entirely, demonstrating that importance-based selection is not merely a marginal improvement but a qualitative necessity at intermediate pruning ratios. On Long, the disruption window shifts to even more conservative ratios: at $k{=}0.925$, where just 7.5\% of tokens are removed, random collapses to 38.6\% while L2 preserves 83.2\%, a 44.6\,pp gap that exceeds the $k{=}0.85$ margin of 28.6\,pp (Figure~\ref{fig:cliff_highlight}). Compositional tasks thus amplify the disruption peak, requiring importance-based selection at progressively smaller removal fractions.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/paper/fig_cliff_highlight.pdf}
\caption{Scoring method determines cliff behavior on $\pi_{0.5}$ Long. L2 norm selection maintains near-baseline SR through the cliff zone ($k{=}0.85$--$0.95$), while random pruning collapses at $k{=}0.925$ (44.6\,pp gap). At $k{\leq}0.7$, random recovers, closing the gap. The cliff zone represents a critical regime where scoring method, not compression ratio, determines success or failure.}
\label{fig:cliff_highlight}
\end{figure}

